  "tutorial_example_entry": {
    "lesson_index": 3,
    "lesson_title": "The System-Then-Shift Path",
    "lesson_core_move": "Compute the system-then-shift path by applying the continuous-time system rule to $x(t)$ to get $y(t)$, then replacing t with t - T to form $y(t - T)$ for comparison in a time-invariance test.",
    "section_count": 5,
    "sections": [
      {
        "lesson_index": 3,
        "lesson_title": "The System-Then-Shift Path",
        "section_index": 1,
        "section_title": "Introduction to The System-Then-Shift Path",
        "step_type": "tutorial",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "tutorial_content": {
          "blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The system-then-shift path means you first apply the system rule to the original input $x(t)$ to get a finished output $y(t)$, and then you shift that completed output by replacing every $t$ with $t-T$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "y(t)=A\\,x(t-a)+B",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "That shift acts on the completed expression, not on the input first. So the $t$ inside $x(t-a)$ shifts with the rest of the output, while the constant term $B$ stays the same.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "y(t-T)=A\\,x(t-T-a)+B",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So if you already have $y(t)$, the system-then-shift path is just $y(t-T)$. This is the output that will later be compared with the result of shifting the input first in a time-invariance test.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 3,
        "lesson_title": "The System-Then-Shift Path",
        "section_index": 2,
        "section_title": "Applying the System Rule Before Any Input Shift",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "For the system $y(t)=2x(t)+x(t-1)$ and input $x(t)=t^2$, find $y(t)$ and then compute $y(t-T)$.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is that the system rule is already given, so the first job is to apply it to the original input $x(t)=t^2$ and form the finished output $y(t)$ before any shift is introduced.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "y(t)=2x(t)+x(t-1)=2t^2+(t-1)^2=3t^2-2t+1",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Now the system-then-shift path shifts the completed output, not the input rule. That means every explicit $t$ in $y(t)$ is replaced by $t-T$, while the constant term stays unchanged.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "y(t-T)=3(t-T)^2-2(t-T)+1",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "This example shows the order clearly: first build $y(t)$ from $x(t)$, then shift that finished expression to get $y(t-T)$. That is the system-then-shift path used later in the time-invariance comparison.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 3,
        "lesson_title": "The System-Then-Shift Path",
        "section_index": 3,
        "section_title": "Forming $y(t - T)$ from a Direct Output Expression",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "If $y(t)=5t^2-2t+1$, write $y(t-T)$.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is that the output is already finished, so the only job is to replace every explicit $t$ in $y(t)$ with $t-T$ and leave constants unchanged.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "y(t-T)=5(t-T)^2-2(t-T)+1",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Now simplify the substituted expression by expanding the square and collecting like terms. The constant $1$ stays fixed because it is not attached to $t$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "y(t-T)=5t^2-10Tt+5T^2-2t+2T+1",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "This is the system-then-shift form for a direct output expression: start with $y(t)$, replace $t$ by $t-T$ everywhere it appears, and then simplify the result.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 3,
        "lesson_title": "The System-Then-Shift Path",
        "section_index": 4,
        "section_title": "Forming $y(t - T)$ When $y(t)$ Contains $x(t - a)$",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "If $y(t)=x(t-2)+3x(t)$, write $y(t-T)$.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is that the output already contains delayed input terms, so the only task is to shift the finished output expression by replacing $t$ with $t-T$ everywhere it appears.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "y(t-T)=x((t-T)-2)+3x(t-T)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The embedded delay stays attached to the shifted time, so $x(t-2)$ becomes $x(t-T-2)$ and the undelayed term $x(t)$ becomes $x(t-T)$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "y(t-T)=x(t-T-2)+3x(t-T)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "This shows the same system-then-shift rule in a delayed-output setting: shift the completed output, keep each delay attached to its argument, and write the result in simplified form.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 3,
        "lesson_title": "The System-Then-Shift Path",
        "section_index": 5,
        "section_title": "Forming $y(t - T)$ When $y(t)$ Combines Multiple Terms",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "If $y(t)=2x(t-1)-x(t)+t+4$, write $y(t-T)$.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is that the output mixes delayed input terms, an undelayed input term, an explicit $t$ term, and a constant, so the shift must be applied to every $t$-dependent part exactly once.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "y(t-T)=2x((t-T)-1)-x(t-T)+(t-T)+4",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The delayed input term becomes $x(t-T-1)$, the undelayed input term becomes $x(t-T)$, the explicit time term becomes $t-T$, and the constant $4$ stays unchanged.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "y(t-T)=2x(t-T-1)-x(t-T)+t-T+4",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "This is the same system-then-shift rule in a mixed-expression setting: shift the finished output once, carry every $t$-bearing term with the shift, and leave constants alone.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      }
    ]
  },
